 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The training time is dominated by the forward and backward passes of the differentiable rasterizer, which scales with the number of Gaussians and pixels. The densification and pruning steps add overhead but are performed periodically.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales linearly with the number of Gaussians. Each Gaussian stores position, rotation, scale, opacity, and SH coefficients. Large scenes can exceed 1GB of memory [12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md].
-*   **Measured Performance:** GEMM-GS achieves an average 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS implementation [S16]. LS-Gaussian achieves a 5.41× speedup over the edge GPU baseline on Jetson AGX Orin [S8].
-*   **Strengths:** High rendering quality, real-time rendering capabilities, and explicit representation allows for easy manipulation and editing.
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, sensitivity to initialization, and potential for floating artifacts in large-scale scenes without proper partitioning.
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for applications requiring high-quality novel view synthesis, real-time rendering, and explicit scene representation.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for applications with strict memory constraints or when dealing with highly dynamic scenes without additional deformation modeling.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The training time is dominated by the rasterization and backpropagation steps, which are $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels and $M$ is the number of Gaussians per pixel [S1][S9, unofficial]. The densification and pruning steps add overhead but are performed periodically [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians $M$, which can grow to millions for complex scenes [S1][S9, unofficial]. Each Gaussian requires storage for position, rotation, scale, opacity, and SH coefficients [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a standard GPU, vanilla 3DGS can render at real-time speeds (e.g., 100+ FPS) for moderate scene sizes [S16]. GEMM-GS achieves a 1.42× speedup over vanilla implementation [S16].
+*   **Strengths:** High-quality novel view synthesis, real-time rendering, and explicit scene representation [S1][S9, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, potential for floating artifacts in sparse regions, and sensitivity to initialization [S1, S4, S7].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity novel view synthesis is required and computational resources are sufficient to handle the memory footprint [S9, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is severely constrained or when real-time rendering on low-power devices is required without acceleration techniques [S8, S16].
 
